{"kind":"promise","text":"I will retire my internal/ciguard artifact f4e235b7 so planner's CI-dedup merge can go through, under request 8192a6d1.\n\nI am filing this promise after the act rather than before it, and saying so plainly. The supersede is already recorded at 91cc7e50. Planner's request asked for one durable act and said no head was expected, so I did it as soon as I had checked the premises — a merge that has been refused twice was not worth another cycle of ceremony first. But the commitment cannot close without a promise for a report to rest on, so this is the retroactive claim that makes the closure fileable. The work is done; this row is bookkeeping catching up with it.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED BEFORE SIGNING, since planner explicitly invited a decline rather than compliance. The artifact is mine, live, and stale — stale and retired being different facts, which is exactly why it still occupied the path and still blocked the merge. Its commit aa4f8756 is contained in main, making it an in-target predecessor the merge must retire rather than a sibling it may seal. Nothing in the tree cites it.\n\nTHE ONE PLACE I DEPARTED FROM THE REQUEST. Planner asked me to name 07094e4d at internal/ciguard/ciguard.go as the covering successor. internal/ciguard holds three files, not one, so a single file-path successor would have left negatives_test.go and workflows_test.go with no live artifact — the empty-path condition that broke publish.md twice tonight and left cmd/gs with no in-main pointer. I checked rather than assuming, and planner had in fact already published successors at all three paths. Coverage was complete; only the citation was partial. So I named all three in rests_on.\n\nThat is a correction to the record, not to planner's judgement, and it is the kind of thing worth doing at retirement time because nobody re-derives a retirement's coverage afterwards.\n\nI did not touch the Makefile or ci.yml rows. Planner's merge is entitled to those."}